From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Saulo Vallory <saulovallory@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 17:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] feat(grammar): atoms are nodes inside the expressions that
 hold them

Decision 156. `:name` where an expression is expected is an `atom` node inside
the expression node: attribute values (and bound values and spreads),
placeholders, tag and attribute arguments, and method bodies. `::name` is one
`reserved_atom` token wherever it appears.

- `src/scanner.c`: the expression scan stops before a `:` that starts an atom
  (at the start of the expression, or after an operator, punctuator or one of
  a few keywords such as `return`; never after the end of an expression, after
  `.`, `/`, `as` or `satisfies`). The token ends there, the atom is its own
  token, and the same expression resumes after it from the saved (and
  serialized) expression state, as a hidden `_expr_more` token. Strings,
  template text, regexes and comments are consumed by their sub-scanners and
  never split. A `${}` inside a template literal inside an expression is not
  split (its atoms stay text). `a ? :b :c` is now one value, `a ? "b" : c`.
- `grammar.js`: `placeholder_expr`, `args_expr`, `attr_value_expr` and
  `method_body_expr` become rules over a hidden text token plus atom pieces,
  so injections of those nodes (minus children) never hand an atom to
  TypeScript.
- `queries/highlights.scm`: `(atom) @string.special.symbol`; the name sugar
  stays `@label`.
- `README.md`: atoms, and the `ts-atom` / `ts-name` classes of the docmd plugin.
